{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Scope (revised 2026-08-13; operator-approved exception to the LOC-remediation freeze)\n\nRe-measured on `origin/main` @ `2af5a4c773`: 435 `src/rebar` `*.py` files, **46 over 700 LOC**,\n7 at 796-800. The two this story named at 799 (`_ref_lock.py`, `init.py`) are now 730/758;\n`_config_schema.py` 677 and `fsck.py` 301 also landed. Worst now: `pass1.py` **800**,\n`_lib_writes.py` **800** (in flight as Gerrit 1737 — OUT OF SCOPE), `completion_banking.py`\n799, `_store/push.py` **799**, `signing.py` 798, `binding_store.py` 798,\n`apply_inbound_records.py` 797, `runner.py` 796.\n\nThis story does **not** drain all 46: per epic `broke-boarish-hagfish` the object is the\nRECURRENCE pattern, not the population. Three changes, one per file-cluster, as a small stack:\nthe two files at/one-line-from the cap not already in flight, plus the leading indicator this\nstory's last AC asks for. `signing.py` was mapped too and deferred to a `discovered_from`\nrecord (see comments): its `_baked_commit_sha` patch-target/consumer pair forces a test edit.\n\n---\n\n## Change 1 — `llm/plan_review/pass1.py` 800 -> ~510\n\nFour fused concerns: (a) the **container (G3/G4) pairing stage**, (b) the driver `run_pass1`,\n(c) ISF/context blob builders (~95 LOC), (d) usage aggregation + material fingerprint (~110).\nOnly (a) is large enough to extract without breaching the 100-LOC floor; (c) and (d) stay with\ntheir nearest collaborator.\n\n**Extract to `llm/plan_review/container_stage.py` (~300 LOC):** `CONTAINER_CRITERIA`,\n`_CONTAINER_MAX_WORKERS`, `_too_big_finding`, `build_sibling_roster`, `_timed_pairing`,\n`_run_container`. Seam: `_run_container` has exactly ONE call site (`run_pass1`'s `if container\nand ctx.has_children:` branch); `_timed_pairing`, `_too_big_finding` and\n`_CONTAINER_MAX_WORKERS` are called from nowhere but `_run_container`. That call site is the\nseam a previous author already cut and stopped at — the shape `defaceable-icy-antelope` found\nin `fsck.py`.\n\n**`_submit_ctx` moves to `generation.py`**, not to the new module: it is shared by BOTH\n`_run_container` and `run_pass1`, so leaving it in `pass1.py` would force\n`container_stage -> pass1` and cycle. `generation.py` is the right home because the worker\n`_submit_ctx` launches calls `generation.review_cancelled()`, and the `_CANCEL_SCOPE` ContextVar\nthat reads is defined in `generation.py:120` — that is the generation-owned state\n`copy_context()` must capture. (The gate-session vars `_submit_ctx` also carries,\n`_in_gate_session` / `_active_code_root`, live in `llm/gate_context.py`, not here; they are\ncaptured generically.) `generation.py`'s docstring already names `pass1._submit_ctx`. Direction\nstays one-way: `pass1 -> container_stage -> generation`. `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` is re-derived\nin `container_stage.py` from `capabilities.CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` — the same single source\n`pass1.py` reads, not a second literal.\n\nCompatibility (semantic search AND string grep, both run): `orchestrator.py` imports\n`CONTAINER_CRITERIA`, `_run_container`, `_ticket_graph_blob`, `material_fingerprint` from\n`.pass1`; `provider_parity.py` imports `CONTAINER_CRITERIA` — both preserved by re-export. The ONLY symbol patched on the `pass1` module is `_shed_to_budget`\n(`test_plan_review.py:2049`), whose consumer `run_pass1` STAYS, so the flamingo trap does not\narise; `_submit_ctx`, `_run_container` and `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` are patched nowhere. Two\nsource-text tests are satisfied by the re-export lines themselves:\n`test_container_roster.py::test_roster_builder_single_sourced` and\n`test_cache_floor_warning.py`. The only complexity-baseline key for this file is\n`pass1.py::run_pass1` (20), which does NOT move: no new key, no re-key.\n\n## Change 2 — `_store/push.py` 799 -> ~500\n\nConcerns: (A) `_git`/`_GIT_TIMEOUT` 15 LOC, (B) **stash / dirty-tree recovery** 126, (C)\n**non-fast-forward recovery** 166, (D) `_push_mode` 15, (E) S3 preflight 30, (F) commit path\n114, (G) entry/driver/CLI 330. B and C form ONE subtree whose only edge into the rest is\n`push_tickets_branch`'s single call to `_recover_non_fast_forward`; every other member\n(`_stash_create`, `_restore_stash`, `_resolve_conflicted_apply`, `_recover_dirty_merge`,\n`_merge_with_transport_retry`, `_merge_remote_under_lock`, `_fetch_for_recovery`) has one\ncaller, inside the subtree. **Extract B+C + `_PUSH_MERGE_LOCK_TIMEOUT` to\n`_store/push_recovery.py` (~345 LOC).** A/D/E are far under the 100-LOC floor, staying with\n`push_tickets_branch`; F is deliberately NOT cut — 114 LOC barely clears the floor and would\nneed a second late-binding seam for no headroom gain.\n\nThe hazard: `push._git` is monkeypatched at ~25 sites across 10 test modules and 8 of the 9\nmoved functions call it. A module-level `from .push import _git` in the extracted file\nwould both cycle and bind pre-patch, silently running REAL git while tests assert on a fake.\nRemedy is the project's own established pattern, `_ref_lock_push.py`: each moved entry point\ntakes the calling module as its first `core` parameter and resolves `core._git` and\n`core.logger` at call time. `_git`, `_GIT_TIMEOUT` and `logger` all stay in `push.py`, so\n`push_state.py`'s `from rebar._store.push import _git`, the `rebar._store.push` logger-name\nassertions in 6 test modules (including the store-epoch warning emitted from the MOVED\n`_merge_remote_under_lock`), and `_bridge_runner.py`'s `python -m rebar._store.push` keep\nworking unchanged. `push_recovery.py` imports nothing from `push.py` — one-way.\n\n`test_push_shared_stash_2fa6.py` calls `_push._recover_dirty_merge(...)` directly with\n`push._git` patched; it gets a two-line delegating shim in `push.py`, not a test edit. `compat`\n/ `lock` stay imported AS MODULES on both sides so their patch sites survive.\n`.github/complexity-baseline.json` has zero keys for `push.py`.\n\n## Change 3 — surface the leading indicator that already exists\n\nDo NOT build a second indicator (per the 2026-08-05 comment): `near_cap_count` is computed and\nreports 37, but only someone running `rebar metrics` ever sees it. The CI **Module-size gate**\nalready computes per-file LOC for every file it measures. Append to that\nsame step a NON-BLOCKING pass emitting one `::warning::` per `src/rebar` file within a headroom\nband of the locked cap, exiting 0 regardless. Reuses the gate's own `find | wc -l` — no second\ndefinition of the rule, no new file, no new failure mode, band derived from the locked limit. One edit: the gate lives only in the reusable `_build-and-test.yml`, which\nboth `gerrit-verify.yaml` and `test.yml` call. Policy note in `docs/architecture.md`.\n\n## Verification, per change, before push\n\nFull CI-parity suite (`-m \"not integration and not external\" -n 3 --dist worksteal\n--timeout=300`) plus `tests/integration`, with **test-count parity** against the parent commit\nper split; every `check_*.py` gate; every `gen_*.py` generator re-run with no diff; `make lint`;\n`make typecheck`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `pass1.py` <= 550 LOC; `container_stage.py` in [100, 500]; `generation.py` <= 500.\n- [x] `push.py` <= 550 LOC; `push_recovery.py` in [100, 500]. No file created by this story is\n      under 100 LOC or over 550.\n- [x] `pass1.<symbol>` / `push.<symbol>` attribute access preserved for every symbol named\n      above; `orchestrator.py`, `provider_parity.py`, `push_state.py`, `_bridge_runner.py` and\n      `_init_probe.py` byte-unchanged.\n- [x] Zero test files edited by Changes 1 and 2; test-count parity demonstrated per change.\n- [x] `check_complexity_baseline.py --lock` OK on each change; the baseline file unmodified.\n- [x] The CI module-size gate emits a non-blocking near-cap warning listing every file in the\n      headroom band, and still passes when that list is non-empty.\n- [x] `docs/architecture.md` records both splits and the near-cap warning.\n- [x] [operator-attested] Each change earns `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1` and is submitted\n      before this ticket closes; the Gerrit change numbers are recorded as ticket comments.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786670386670241001,"uuid":"5493e737-cdb1-4f76-a8f4-86f87ec4bf8e"}